WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.799
The first thing you need to know is that
Palestinian leadership is divided.

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:18.159
On one side there's Hamas. They control
the Gaza Strip.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:21.920
It's their military wing that was firing
some of the rockets at Israel.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.040
The US and Israel consider Hamas
terrorists.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.759
In Gaza Hamas is led by Yahya Sinwar.
Then there's the Palestinian Authority.

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:33.600
The PA was set up in 1994 and it's
effectively like the Palestinian

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.799
government
but today with Hamas in control of Gaza

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:40.880
the PA's control is very limited
except for parts of the West Bank and

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.799
even there it's Israel,
as the occupying power, that has control

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:49.119
over most things.
Since 2005 the PA has been controlled by

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:52.479
Fatah
a political party led by Mahmoud Abbas.

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:55.759
He's the Palestinian president
and represents Palestine on the

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:59.040
international stage.
This split between Hamas and the

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.119
Palestinian Authority creates
all sorts of problems and raises the

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:07.119
question of who
actually represents the Palestinians.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.160
The PA was going to hold elections in
May but Mahmoud Abbas cancelled them at

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.800
the last minute.
He said it was because of Israeli

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.800
restrictions on campaigning and voting
in occupied East Jerusalem.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:33.519
But some Palestinians question if that's
the whole story.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.720
Now, this political division has existed
for a long time

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.640
but it now seems there's another centre
of political power that's getting

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.040
stronger

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:16.959
and that's the power of the Palestinian
people themselves

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:23.440
on the streets and across social media.

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:52.959
So that's an overview of the
situation on the Palestinian side.

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:56.879
The second thing you need to know is
that on the Israeli side politics are

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:01.760
also in a mess. In March Israel held its
fourth election in two years without a

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.680
clear result.
There were weeks of deal making and now

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.680
Benjamin Netanyahu is being pushed out
after 12 years in power.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.159
His opponents formed a coalition with
Naftali Bennett — a far-right politician

00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:17.840
set to be the new prime minister. He's
supported by Yair Lapid

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.600
a centrist who's expected to take over
as prime minister in two years.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.840
So how does all this play into the
Israeli-Palestinian issue?

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.880
Well, of course, it's hard to say
definitively. The issue

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.320
hasn't come up much in any of the recent
election campaigns

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.080
and most Israelis support keeping things
with the Palestinians

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.720
pretty much as they are. But here's what
we know about the new leaders.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.440
OK, so on the Palestinian side we've
got a major political split and on the

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.199
Israeli side we've got
chronic political uncertainty even with

00:05:37.199 --> 00:05:40.560
the new coalition.
It doesn't exactly look great when it

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.720
comes to finding the kind of
committed — maybe even visionary — political

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.360
action needed to bring
lasting peace.

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:10.560
Now the other big factor in all this is
the US's relationship with Israel

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.800
because the third thing you need to know
is that the US has always been Israel's

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.840
biggest supporter.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.240
For Palestinians it often feels like
this friendship comes at their expense

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.840
and under President Trump it reached a
whole new level.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.840
He recognised Jerusalem as the capital
of Israel and moved the US embassy

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.199
there from Tel Aviv.
This ignored Palestinian claims to East

00:06:59.199 --> 00:07:03.360
Jerusalem as their capital.
He pulled more than $200 million

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.120
in aid to the Palestinian Authority
and the US had opposed Israeli

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.919
settlements but Trump reversed that
policy.

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:13.440
So going forward the question is how the
Biden administration

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.520
might do things differently. And so far
there has been a change of tone

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.240
compared to the Trump years. 

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.120
But when Israel started bombing Gaza in
May it took

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.639
eight days for Biden to express support
for a ceasefire.

00:07:34.639 --> 00:07:39.039
The US also blocked three attempts by
the UN Security Council to condemn the

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:42.000
violence
and around the same time news broke

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.560
about a US weapons sale to Israel
worth $735 million.

00:07:58.319 --> 00:08:02.160
But when it comes
to US support for Israel

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.919
we've recently seen a lot more open
criticism.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.399
But does the US leadership
have any appetite to really deal with

00:08:34.399 --> 00:08:38.479
the underlying issues?

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:50.720
The
thing is

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.160
it seems that it's never been the right
time for peace.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.399
But from Palestine to the US to cities
around the world

00:08:58.399 --> 00:09:02.480
the voices calling for some kind of
change are getting louder.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:08.000
For politicians the pressure is on.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.959
If you want to know more about some of
the issues that caused the violence we

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:24.320
saw in May check out our episode on how
the fighting between Israelis and

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.920
Palestinians got so bad.
You can find that and other Start Here

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.560
content on YouTube and our social media
pages.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:37.839
See you next week.

